  "query_preview": "..  (Sunday Night through Friday)\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nContinued southerly flow throughout the entire column on Monday will \nlead to steadily increasing warm/moist advection from the Gulf. As a \nresult, highs on Monday will be similar to Sunday, even with more \ncloud cover, with most locations reaching the upper 80s once again. \nA baroclinic zone is progged by guidance to generally be positioned \nnear central KS northeastward into WI Monday into Tuesday, with \nwarm/moist flow on the equatorward side and cool/drier flow on the \npoleward side. The longwave trough begins to shift eastward as weak \nshortwaves propagate out ahead of it within the southwesterly flow. \nAs these features near the region and interact with our warm/moist \nairmass, chances for showers and thunderstorms increase on Monday. \nHowever, a majority of the shower and thunderstorm activity during \nthis timeframe should remain closely attached to this zone, keeping \nthe best precipitation chances (70-80%) across northern MO and \npoints to the north on Sunday night into Monday.\n\nBy Monday night, a shortwave embedded within the longwave trough \nejects into the Great Plains triggering lee-side surface \ncyclogenesis across the Central Plains/Midwest. This surface low is \nmodeled to lift northeast, pulling the baroclinic zone southward as \na cold front towards the area on Tuesday. Our greatest severe \nweather threat will be as this cold front pushes through the area, \nbringing with it the strongest wind shear and forcing for ascent to \nsustain convection. Currently, guidance is in fairly good agreement \nthat this SW-NE oriented front will be approaching northeast MO/west-\ncentral IL Tuesday afternoon, with slow progression through the CWA \nTuesday evening into Wednesday morning. A FROPA that favors the \ndiurnal instability maximum could enhance the severe weather threat, \nwhile an overnight FROPA might limit the severe weather threat \nslightly. Key details like the exact timing, magnitude, and \norientation of this cold front remain to be fine tuned, all of which \nwill play a big role in the convective strength and mode of storms. \nThese key detail will continue to be refined as long-range guidance \nhones in on a general solution. The main takeaway is that multiple \nchances for showers and thunderstorms exist along and ahead of this \nfront, with the best chance for a more widespread severe weather \nthreat existing when the cold front arrives, which will be fine \ntuned with future forecasts.\n\nCooler conditions are forecast to filter in behind this cold front, \nalong with prevailing drier weather Wednesday into next weekend. \nAnalysis of the LREF temperature IQRs reveal this well as IQRs on \nWednesday are centered on values about 10-15 degrees cooler than \nTuesday. By the end of the week, temperature IQR spreads increase, \nsignally greater uncertainty, with the general trend being a gradual \nwarmup into next weekend to a return of near average temperatures.\n\nPeine",
